<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Capacocha</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Qhapaq hucha, Qhapaq ucha, Capac cocha, Capaccocha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Capacocha (Qhapaq hucha) was a sacrificial rite of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> that typically involved the sacrifice of children, sent as tribute by communities from across the empire and offered at important shrines known as huacas. Ceremonies marked events in the life of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a>, such as his accession, illness or death, or were held to stop natural disasters; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacuti">Pachacuti</a> was the first Sapa Inca to order one. The children were paraded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a>, then taken to the shrines, often on high Andean peaks, where they were killed or died of cold. Mummies found on Llullaillaco in 1999 and on Ampato, among them the girl called Juanita, preserve the rite's victims and offerings.</p></div>
